{
  "extraction_note": "Interface polylines are extracted by walking the element chain of the free-surface line mesh, not by sorting nodes on z. The rear meniscus is re-entrant at the highest capillary numbers in this sweep, so a z-sort cuts across the corner and reports a profile the solver never held. The reported films are unaffected, because they are measured as a median over the flat central film, which does not depend on point order; the plotted profiles were affected, and are the reason the extraction was rewritten.",
  "interface_profiles_note": "Converged interface polylines are vendored beside this receipt so the figure is reproducible from the checkout alone. The live run wrote them under its registered working-storage run path; that absolute path is deliberately not retained here, because a committed receipt that only resolves on one machine is not a receipt.",
  "merged_note": "Two ladders merged. Ca_in 1e-4 to 0.7 were each solved independently from their own seeded capsule; Ca_in 1 to 6 were reached by continuation from the Ca_in = 0.7 solve, which is therefore both the last independent point and the ladder's seed. The 'route' field on each row records which, because continued points are not independent evidence of one another; verification/s10_route.py measures the two routes agreeing to 8.2e-5 in the film and 9.1e-5 in the bubble speed where both of them work.",
  "provenance_note": "Built in two passes: the independent ladder (Ca_in 1e-4 to 0.5) with runners/sweep.py --seed-from-bubble-speed, then the continuation ladder (Ca_in 0.7 to 6) with --continuation --seed-from-bubble-speed --merge, both at --pad-rear 1.5 --pad-front 3.0 and with --output-root on the registered working volume. The interface_profile paths are then rewritten from that volume to the vendored copies beside this receipt, which is the one step the sweep driver does not do: it records where it wrote, and a committed receipt has to point at something inside the checkout.",
